{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786210152,"nonce":"+dyah+77Ff5vz74PAeM30FCwbb8=","target":"63e91ed3e3b33a54b434e4ef9639291acb3674996efe4a1521623c70299bd58a","message":"**What is wrong.** `insecure_skip_verify` is a per-channel boolean inside the\ntenant-writable webhook config blob\n(`internal/channels/providers/webhook/config.go:31-33`) and is honoured directly\nat `provider.go:161-164`. Any org member can disable TLS certificate verification\nfor their own webhook channel through the public `POST /api/v1/channels`. Unlike\nthe sources path — where `tls_skip_verify` only takes effect if the operator sets\nthe server-level `security.allow_insecure_tls`, which defaults to false\n(`internal/platform/config/config.go:194-201, 288`) — the webhook provider has no\nsuch gate. Reported as M2 in the security review and not addressed by `e776716`,\nwhich fixed the SSRF findings around it.\n\n**Why it matters.** Notification payloads carry alert labels, annotations and the\nrule expression — a description of what is broken inside the customer's\ninfrastructure. A tenant-set flag that turns off certificate verification makes\nthat traffic trivially interceptable, and it is set by whoever configures a\nchannel, not by whoever owns the security posture. The asymmetry with sources also\nmeans the operator's `allow_insecure_tls: false` is not the control they think it\nis.\n\n**Where.** `internal/channels/providers/webhook/config.go:31-33`,\n`internal/channels/providers/webhook/provider.go:161-164`,\n`internal/platform/config/config.go:194-201`.\n\n**Done when.** The webhook provider honours the same server-level\n`security.allow_insecure_tls` gate as sources: with it false the per-channel flag\nis rejected at validation with a clear message, and the served JSON Schema does\nnot offer a control the deployment will not honour. A test covers both settings.","files":null}]}